BANK.push(
{"id":"o1","d":6,"q":"A national retailer's SIEM ingests 4 TB of logs per day, and licensing costs are growing faster than the security budget. Analysts say most of the data is never used in detections or investigations. The CISO asks the architect to cut costs without losing investigative capability. Which design is BEST?","o":["Tiered storage: hot SIEM for detection, cheaper store for bulk","Stop collecting everything except firewall and antivirus logs","Cut SIEM retention to seven days for every source","Keep sending everything but randomly sample 10% of each source"],"a":[0],"e":"Tiered logging keeps detection-critical data hot and moves bulk data to cheaper storage that can still be searched for investigations. Dropping sources creates blind spots, very short retention destroys investigation capability (dwell time is often weeks), and random sampling loses the exact events you need."},
{"id":"o2","d":6,"q":"During a ransomware incident, the incident response team discovered that the attacker had been in the network for 47 days. The organization keeps endpoint telemetry for 14 days and firewall logs for 30 days. What is the MOST important architecture lesson?","o":["Log retention must reflect realistic attacker dwell times and investigation needs","The organization needs additional firewalls at the perimeter and between internal network segments","The endpoint agent should be replaced with another vendor's product that has better detection","Telemetry retention is irrelevant because the organization had good backups to restore from"],"a":[0],"e":"Without logs covering the full dwell time, you can't determine initial access, scope, or what data was taken. Retention should be set from investigation requirements and threat intelligence on dwell times, often 90 days hot plus a year or more warm."},
{"id":"o3","d":6,"q":"A security operations team wants to automate repetitive tasks such as enriching alerts with threat intelligence, disabling compromised accounts, and isolating endpoints. Which technology is designed for this?","o":["SOAR playbooks across tools","A larger SIEM license with more correlation rules and more storage for enrichment data","A network intrusion detection system","A vulnerability scanner"],"a":[0],"e":"SOAR platforms run playbooks that orchestrate actions across tools, reducing response time and analyst workload. SIEM detects, IDS monitors network traffic, and scanners find vulnerabilities."},
{"id":"o4","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing automated containment playbooks. Which design consideration is MOST important to avoid causing business outages?","o":["Automate low-impact, high-confidence actions; require human approval for high-impact ones","Automate every response action without exception, since speed is the most important factor in limiting damage","Never automate any response action; all actions must be performed manually by analysts","Run automated actions only on weekends, when business impact is lowest"],"a":[0],"e":"Automation can cause outages if it acts on false positives against critical assets. Graduated automation balances speed and safety. All-or-nothing approaches either risk outages or lose speed."},
{"id":"o5","d":6,"q":"A company's business impact analysis sets an RTO of 4 hours and an RPO of 1 hour for its order management system. The current design takes nightly backups and can restore within 6 hours. Which statement is correct?","o":["It fails both: up to 24 hours of data loss and a 6-hour restore","It meets the RPO but not the RTO, since nightly backups lose at most one hour of transactions","It meets the RTO but not the RPO, since six hours is within the overall recovery window","It meets both objectives, since the backups are complete and the restore is tested"],"a":[0],"e":"RPO is the maximum acceptable data loss (time since last good copy); nightly backups give up to 24 hours. RTO is the maximum time to restore; 6 hours exceeds 4. Both fail."},
{"id":"o6","d":6,"q":"Which statement BEST describes the relationship between maximum tolerable downtime (MTD), RTO, and work recovery time (WRT)?","o":["RTO plus WRT must be less than or equal to MTD","RTO must always be greater than MTD so that there is a safety margin for recovery","WRT is another name for the recovery point objective","MTD is the time needed to restore data from backup"],"a":[0],"e":"RTO covers restoring systems; WRT covers verifying data and resuming business processes. Their sum must fit within MTD, the point beyond which the business suffers unacceptable harm."},
{"id":"o7","d":6,"q":"An organization is designing its disaster recovery strategy. For a critical system with a 15-minute RTO, which recovery site type is MOST appropriate?","o":["A hot site or active-active deployment","A cold site with power, cooling, and space available, where equipment is shipped and installed after a disaster is declared","A warm site restored from weekly backups","A reciprocal agreement with another company"],"a":[0],"e":"A 15-minute RTO needs systems already running with current data. Cold and warm sites take hours to days, and reciprocal agreements are unreliable and hard to test."},
{"id":"o8","d":6,"q":"An organization tests its disaster recovery plan by having team members talk through their roles in a simulated scenario in a meeting room. Which type of test is this?","o":["A tabletop exercise (structured walk-through)","A full interruption test","A parallel test","A simulation test with actual failover"],"a":[0],"e":"Tabletop exercises discuss responses without touching systems. Parallel tests run recovery systems alongside production, and full interruption tests actually shut down primary systems: the most realistic and the riskiest."},
{"id":"o9","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing the security operations center (SOC) for a company that operates 24x7 but has budget for only six analysts. Which model is MOST appropriate?","o":["Hybrid: MDR for 24x7 triage, internal team for escalation","Six analysts on rotating 24x7 shifts with no external support","Business-hours monitoring, reviewing overnight alerts each morning","Outsource everything, including incident decisions, to the provider"],"a":[0],"e":"Six people can't staff 24x7 sustainably (it takes roughly 10-12 for one seat around the clock). Hybrid models give round-the-clock coverage, while internal staff keep business context and decision authority. Business-hours-only leaves long gaps."},
{"id":"o10","d":6,"q":"An architect is improving detection coverage. The team wants to know which attacker techniques its current controls can detect and where gaps exist. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Map detections to MITRE ATT&CK and validate with adversary emulation or purple teaming","Count the SIEM rules in each category and compare the totals with industry benchmarks","Buy a new tool for each ATT&CK tactic","Review vendor marketing material for each tool"],"a":[0],"e":"ATT&CK mapping shows which techniques are covered on paper, and emulation verifies the detections actually fire. Rule counts don't show coverage, and buying tools without analysis wastes money."},
{"id":"o11","d":6,"q":"A company's threat intelligence program receives dozens of feeds containing millions of indicators. Analysts are overwhelmed by alerts on low-value indicators. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["Requirements-driven, curated, aged intelligence","Subscribe to additional commercial and open-source feeds so that coverage of attacker infrastructure is as complete as possible","Block every indicator from every feed at the firewall","Stop using threat intelligence"],"a":[0],"e":"Intelligence should be driven by requirements (who targets us, how). Curation and aging reduce noise. More feeds add noise, blanket blocking causes outages from false positives, and abandoning intelligence loses value."},
{"id":"o12","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing the incident response capability. Which element should be defined FIRST in the incident response plan?","o":["Roles, responsibilities, and authority","The forensic tools and hardware to be purchased for the incident response team's lab","The format and color scheme of incident reports","Which vendors to call for marketing support"],"a":[0],"e":"Clear authority prevents paralysis during incidents. Tools matter but come after governance. NIST SP 800-61 emphasizes preparation, including roles and communication."},
{"id":"o13","d":6,"q":"During an incident, the response team must decide whether to immediately contain an attacker or monitor them to gather intelligence. What is the MOST important factor in this decision?","o":["Ongoing harm versus intelligence value","The analyst's interest in learning the attacker's techniques","Whether the attacker is using a well-known malware family","The time of day the incident was discovered"],"a":[0],"e":"Monitoring an active attacker can be valuable but risks further damage. The decision must be risk-based and made by someone with authority, often involving legal, especially if law enforcement is involved."},
{"id":"o14","d":6,"q":"A forensic analyst must collect evidence from a running server suspected of compromise. In which order should data be collected according to the order of volatility?","o":["Memory and live state first, then disk, then remote logs","Disk first, then memory, then network connections","Backups first, then disk, then memory","Remote logs first, then disk, then memory"],"a":[0],"e":"RFC 3227: collect the most volatile data first, since it disappears soonest. Memory and live state are lost on shutdown, while disk and remote logs persist longer."},
{"id":"o15","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing forensic readiness. Which capability is MOST important for ensuring evidence will be admissible in court?","o":["Chain of custody, hash-based integrity verification, and trained staff using validated tools","Use the most expensive commercial forensic software available, since courts recognize leading products","Store all evidence in a cloud storage bucket","Allow any administrator to collect evidence"],"a":[0],"e":"Admissibility depends on showing evidence wasn't altered and was handled properly: chain of custody, hashing, and competent, documented procedures. Tool price and storage location are secondary."},
{"id":"o16","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing time synchronization for the logging architecture. Why is this critical?","o":["Accurate, consistent timestamps are needed to correlate events and build reliable timelines","Time synchronization improves network performance by reducing retransmissions between hosts","It is only required for Kerberos authentication","It reduces log storage requirements"],"a":[0],"e":"Without synchronized clocks, correlating events across systems is unreliable and evidence can be challenged. NTP or PTP from trusted, authenticated sources is foundational (Kerberos also needs it, but that isn't the only reason)."},
{"id":"o17","d":6,"q":"A company's vulnerability management program scans all assets monthly and produces thousands of findings. Remediation teams can't keep up. Which approach BEST prioritizes remediation?","o":["Risk-based prioritization (KEV, EPSS)","Fix vulnerabilities strictly in order of CVSS base score, highest first, regardless of where the affected system sits","Fix them in alphabetical order by hostname","Fix the oldest vulnerabilities first"],"a":[0],"e":"CVSS alone over-prioritizes many issues that are never exploited. Adding exploit likelihood (EPSS), known exploitation (KEV), and business context focuses effort on real risk."},
{"id":"o18","d":6,"q":"An organization's patch management process requires a 30-day testing period for every patch. A critical vulnerability is being actively exploited in the wild. What should the architecture support?","o":["An emergency change path with compensating controls","Wait the full 30 days and follow the normal process","Skip patches on critical systems to avoid disruption","Deploy without any testing to all systems at once"],"a":[0],"e":"Normal processes must allow risk-based acceleration. Emergency procedures balance speed and stability, and compensating controls (WAF rules, isolation) protect while patching proceeds. Waiting 30 days during active exploitation is unacceptable."},
{"id":"o19","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing change management for production systems. Which control BEST detects unauthorized changes?","o":["Configuration monitoring that compares actual state against baselines and change tickets","A weekly change advisory board meeting that reviews and approves every proposed change to production systems","Change requests submitted by email","An annual audit of the change log"],"a":[0],"e":"Detective controls must compare reality against approved changes. CAB meetings and request processes are preventive and only cover changes people submit; unauthorized changes bypass them. Annual audits are too infrequent."},
{"id":"o20","d":6,"q":"An organization deploys EDR on all endpoints. Attackers in a recent incident disabled the EDR agent before running ransomware. Which design improvement MOST directly addresses this?","o":["Tamper protection and agent-health alerting","Install a second antivirus product alongside the EDR","Increase the EDR's scan frequency","Remove EDR from endpoints, since it can be disabled by attackers and gives a false sense of security"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers commonly target security tools. Tamper protection, alerting on loss of telemetry, and removing admin rights make disabling harder and noisier. A second AV may conflict and can also be disabled."},
{"id":"o21","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing a security monitoring strategy for a cloud environment. Which log source is MOST critical for detecting malicious activity in the cloud control plane?","o":["Cloud API audit logs","Operating system logs from every virtual machine, forwarded through an agent installed in the base image","Application debug logs","Load balancer access logs"],"a":[0],"e":"Control plane activity (creating users, changing policies, disabling logging) is captured in cloud API audit logs. OS, application, and load balancer logs don't capture control plane changes."},
{"id":"o22","d":6,"q":"A company wants to detect insider threats, such as employees exfiltrating data before resigning. Which capability is MOST effective?","o":["UEBA with HR context signals","Signature-based antivirus on all endpoints, updated hourly, to catch the tools used for exfiltration","Perimeter firewall logs only","Annual security awareness training"],"a":[0],"e":"Insider threats use legitimate access, so behavioral baselines and context (HR events) are key. Signatures don't detect legitimate tool use, and firewall logs alone lack user context. Programs must respect privacy and employment law."},
{"id":"o23","d":6,"q":"An incident response team needs to communicate securely during a major incident in which the corporate email and chat systems may be compromised. What should the IR architecture include?","o":["Pre-arranged out-of-band channels","Corporate email with S/MIME encryption","Updates posted on the company intranet","Personal social media accounts"],"a":[0],"e":"If the attacker controls email or chat, they can monitor the response. Pre-arranged out-of-band channels separate from compromised infrastructure are essential. Encrypted corporate email is still on compromised systems."},
{"id":"o24","d":6,"q":"A company is designing a deception capability. Which design element provides HIGH-fidelity detection with very few false positives?","o":["Honeytokens and decoy credentials","Full packet capture of all network traffic, retained for 30 days and indexed for searching","More SIEM rules","Hourly vulnerability scans"],"a":[0],"e":"Legitimate users have no reason to use decoy credentials or files, so any use is a strong signal of malicious activity. Volume-based approaches create noise."},
{"id":"o25","d":6,"q":"An architect is defining metrics for the SOC. Which pair of metrics BEST reflects the SOC's effectiveness at limiting attacker impact?","o":["Mean time to detect and to contain","Number of alerts generated and number of tickets closed per analyst each month","Number of analysts and tools","Number of firewall rules and IDS signatures"],"a":[0],"e":"Faster detection and containment directly reduce attacker dwell time and impact. Alert and ticket counts measure workload, and staffing or tool counts are inputs."},
{"id":"o26","d":6,"q":"A manufacturing company's SOC monitors only the IT network. The plant-floor OT network has no monitoring, and the OT team worries that active scanning or agents could disrupt production lines. The board wants visibility into OT threats after an industry peer suffered a ransomware attack that spread to OT. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Passive ICS-aware monitoring feeding the SOC","Install the IT EDR agent on every PLC and HMI with auto-isolation","Weekly authenticated scans of the OT network during production","Connect OT to IT so existing tools can see it"],"a":[0],"e":"Passive monitoring sees OT traffic without touching fragile devices, and protocol-aware tools understand Modbus, DNP3, S7, and similar. OT engineers are needed to judge safety impact. Agents often aren't supported on PLCs, auto-isolation can stop production, active scans can crash devices, and merging networks increases exposure."},
{"id":"o27","d":6,"q":"A financial firm's incident response plan states that the general counsel must approve any notification to regulators. During a weekend incident, the general counsel couldn't be reached for 36 hours, and the firm missed a 24-hour reporting deadline. What is the BEST architectural fix?","o":["Delegated authority and alternates for key roles","Remove the requirement for legal approval of regulatory notifications","Let SOC analysts notify regulators directly","Ask regulators to extend deadlines for incidents that happen at weekends"],"a":[0],"e":"Single points of failure in people are as real as in technology. Delegation and alternates keep the process running while preserving oversight. Removing legal review or letting analysts notify directly risks inaccurate or premature disclosures."},
{"id":"o28","d":6,"q":"A company runs its own data center and is building a continuity plan for a pandemic scenario in which up to 40% of staff are unavailable for weeks. Which planning element is MOST important?","o":["Identify critical roles, cross-train staff, document procedures, and enable secure remote work","Buy additional server hardware and keep it in storage at the secondary site for rapid deployment","Increase the number of backup tapes","Install additional fire suppression"],"a":[0],"e":"Pandemic scenarios are about people, not facilities or hardware. Cross-training, documentation, and remote work capability keep critical functions running with reduced staff."},
{"id":"o29","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing the collection architecture for endpoint logs from 30,000 remote laptops that are often off the corporate network. Which design is MOST appropriate?","o":["Cloud collection over TLS with local buffering","Collect logs only when laptops connect to the corporate VPN","Ask users to upload log files once a week","Collect logs only from laptops while they are in the office"],"a":[0],"e":"Remote endpoints need internet-reachable, authenticated collection with local buffering. VPN-only or office-only collection creates gaps, and manual uploads are unreliable."},
{"id":"o30","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing detection for credential misuse in Active Directory. Which detection approach is MOST effective against attackers using stolen but valid credentials?","o":["Behavioral anomaly detections plus deception","Alert on every failed login","Alert on every successful login","Block all logins outside business hours, with exceptions for staff who are on call"],"a":[0],"e":"Valid credentials produce successful logins, so you need anomaly detection based on behavior. Failed logins miss successful misuse, alerting on every login is noise, and time-based blocking disrupts work while missing daytime misuse."},
{"id":"o31","d":6,"q":"A company's backups are tested by verifying that backup jobs complete successfully. During a real incident, restores failed because of corrupted backup catalogs. What is the BEST improvement?","o":["Regular, real test restores","Run backup jobs more frequently, so that each job is smaller and less likely to fail partway through","Buy a more expensive backup product","Increase backup retention"],"a":[0],"e":"Backup success doesn't mean restore success. Only actual restore testing validates recoverability. Frequency, product, and retention don't address untested restores."},
{"id":"o32","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing threat hunting capabilities. Which statement BEST describes threat hunting?","o":["A proactive, hypothesis-driven search for threats that evaded automated detection","Responding to alerts raised by the SIEM, EDR, and other detection tools in priority order","Running vulnerability scans","Reviewing firewall rules"],"a":[0],"e":"Threat hunting assumes compromise and actively looks for evidence that automated tools missed, often guided by threat intelligence or ATT&CK techniques. Responding to alerts is triage, and scanning and rule review are other activities."},
{"id":"o33","d":6,"q":"A security architect must decide where to store SIEM data for a company subject to data residency laws in several countries. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Regional storage with federated search or masking","Send all logs to one central SIEM in the headquarters country","Disable logging in countries with residency laws","Keep logs only on local devices"],"a":[0],"e":"Logs often contain personal data subject to residency. Regional storage with federated search or minimization meets the law while allowing global monitoring. Disabling logging or local-only storage kills visibility."},
{"id":"o34","d":6,"q":"After a major incident, the organization conducts a post-incident review. What is the PRIMARY purpose of this activity?","o":["Find root causes and lessons learned to improve controls, detection, and response, in a blameless format","Determine which employee caused the incident so that appropriate disciplinary action can be taken","Close the ticket quickly","Document it for insurance only"],"a":[0],"e":"Post-incident reviews drive improvement. Blameless formats encourage honest reporting. Discipline may be separately appropriate for misconduct, but that isn't the review's purpose."},
{"id":"o35","d":6,"q":"A company wants to ensure that security monitoring continues during a major data center outage. Which design consideration is MOST important?","o":["Keep monitoring infrastructure out of the failure domain it watches","Place the SIEM in the same data center as production for low latency and simpler network design","Rely on email alerts from the production mail server","Turn monitoring off during outages"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers may cause or exploit outages. Monitoring must survive the failures it's watching. Colocating it with production or depending on production email creates a shared failure."},
{"id":"o36","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing the response to a cloud credential compromise in which an attacker created new IAM users and access keys. Which containment action should be prioritized FIRST?","o":["Disable compromised credentials and attacker-created identities and keys, and kill sessions, while preserving logs","Delete all cloud resources in the account","Wait for the attacker to leave","Change the root account's email address only, so the attacker can't use password recovery to regain access"],"a":[0],"e":"Containment means cutting off the attacker's access paths, including persistence they created. Preserving logs supports investigation. Deleting everything destroys evidence and services, and waiting allows more damage."},
{"id":"o37","d":6,"q":"An organization runs a bug bounty program and receives a report of a critical vulnerability in production. What should the vulnerability handling process ensure?","o":["Validate, fix within SLA, and check for exploitation","Pay the researcher and close the report","Fix the issue without checking for earlier exploitation","Ask the researcher to keep testing production for more issues"],"a":[0],"e":"Handling includes validation, remediation, checking for prior exploitation, and researcher communication. Not fixing defeats the purpose, and unscoped testing beyond the program's rules creates risk."},
{"id":"o38","d":6,"q":"A company's incident response plan has never been tested. Which approach provides a realistic test with the LEAST risk to operations?","o":["A tabletop, then a technical simulation in a test environment, progressing to more realistic exercises","A full interruption test in production on the first attempt, to get the most realistic results possible","No testing until a real incident","A test using real customer data in an unprotected environment"],"a":[0],"e":"Progressive testing builds capability safely. Starting with full interruption risks outages, waiting for a real incident leaves the plan unproven, and real customer data in unprotected environments creates new risk."},
{"id":"o39","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing log integrity controls for audit logs that could be used as evidence. Which control is MOST effective?","o":["Near-real-time forwarding to a separate write-once store with integrity verification","Store logs on the same server that generates them, with file permissions restricting access to administrators","Give all admins delete rights on logs","Compress logs to save space"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers erase logs on compromised hosts. Near-real-time forwarding to a separate, immutable store with integrity checks preserves evidence. Local storage and broad delete rights allow tampering."},
{"id":"o40","d":6,"q":"A security operations architect wants to reduce alert fatigue. Analysts receive 5,000 alerts per day, and 95% are false positives. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Tune detections, correlate alerts into incidents, add context, and retire low-value rules","Hire more analysts to handle the volume","Ignore all low-severity alerts","Disable every detection rule that has ever produced a false positive, and rely on the remaining high-fidelity rules"],"a":[0],"e":"Detection engineering (tuning, correlation, enrichment) improves signal-to-noise. Hiring scales cost, not quality. Ignoring low-severity alerts misses early-stage attacks, and blanket disabling creates gaps."},
{"id":"o41","d":6,"q":"An organization wants to verify that its security controls detect and block common attack techniques continuously. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Breach and attack simulation","An annual penetration test by an external firm, with retesting of any findings after remediation","A quarterly compliance audit","A yearly tabletop exercise"],"a":[0],"e":"BAS tests controls continuously against known techniques, catching regressions from configuration drift. Annual tests, audits, and tabletops are point-in-time or non-technical."},
{"id":"o42","d":6,"q":"A company's digital forensics team needs to analyze a compromised cloud VM. Which approach preserves evidence BEST?","o":["Snapshot disks and capture memory, isolate the VM with network controls, and record hashes and custody","Terminate the VM immediately","Reboot the VM to clear the malware","Log in and run cleanup scripts, then take a snapshot of the cleaned VM for later comparison"],"a":[0],"e":"Cloud forensics relies on snapshots and memory capture before changes. Network isolation contains the threat while preserving state. Terminating or rebooting destroys volatile evidence, and cleanup scripts alter evidence."},
{"id":"o43","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing the escalation matrix for incident response. Which criteria should determine escalation severity?","o":["Business impact, regulatory implications, and the attack's scope and stage","The number of alerts generated by the activity","The analyst's personal judgment, without defined criteria, since experienced analysts know best","The time of day the incident occurred"],"a":[0],"e":"Severity should reflect business impact and legal exposure, with defined criteria so escalation is consistent. Alert counts don't equal impact, and unstructured judgment is inconsistent."},
{"id":"o44","d":6,"q":"A company stores a sensitive customer database in the cloud. The security operations team wants to detect unusual data access. Which data source is MOST valuable?","o":["Data access logs showing who accessed which data, how much, and when","CPU utilization metrics for the database instances, with alerts on sustained spikes","Network bandwidth graphs","Server uptime reports"],"a":[0],"e":"Detecting data misuse needs logs of who accessed what data. Performance metrics may hint at bulk activity but lack identity and object detail."},
{"id":"o45","d":6,"q":"An organization is designing its incident classification scheme. Why is it important to distinguish between an 'event' and an 'incident'?","o":["An event is any observable occurrence; an incident threatens or violates policy and triggers response","They are the same thing","Events are network-related and incidents are host-related, so different teams handle them","Incidents are minor and events are major"],"a":[0],"e":"Most events are benign. Classifying which events are incidents focuses response resources and triggers obligations such as notification timelines."},
{"id":"o46","d":6,"q":"A healthcare network suffered a ransomware attack that encrypted its primary EHR database. Immutable backups exist, but the security team isn't sure when the attacker first gained access, and restoring a backup that contains the attacker's persistence would lead to reinfection. Clinicians are using paper records. What is the BEST recovery approach?","o":["Restore into an isolated clean environment, check for the persistence the investigation found, rebuild credentials, then reconnect in stages","Restore the latest backup directly into production immediately, so clinicians can get back to the EHR as quickly as possible and stop using paper records","Pay the ransom for the decryptor, since decrypting is usually faster than restoring from backups and preserves the most recent data","Wait until the full forensic investigation is complete and the initial access date is known before starting any restoration work at all"],"a":[0],"e":"Restoring blindly can reintroduce the attacker. A clean-room restore with validation, credential resets, and staged reconnection balances speed with safety. Paying the ransom has legal, ethical, and reliability problems, and waiting for full forensics delays care unnecessarily, since investigation and recovery can run in parallel."},
{"id":"o47","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing a SIEM correlation rule to detect password spraying against the organization's cloud IdP. Which pattern BEST indicates password spraying?","o":["Many accounts each with a few failures from the same sources in a short window","One account with hundreds of failed logins from a single IP address within a few minutes, followed by a lockout","A single successful login from a country the user has never logged in from before","A user changing their password shortly after receiving a phishing simulation email"],"a":[0],"e":"Spraying tries a few common passwords across many accounts to avoid lockouts. Many failures on one account is brute force, and a new-country login is an impossible-travel signal."},
{"id":"o48","d":6,"q":"An organization wants to detect data exfiltration to cloud storage services. Which combination of controls is MOST effective?","o":["Tenant restrictions distinguishing corporate from personal instances, with DLP and upload-volume alerts","Block every cloud storage domain, including the corporate tenant, so that no files can be uploaded anywhere outside the network","Monitor CPU usage on endpoints for signs of compression or encryption of large files before upload","Review printer logs for large print jobs that may indicate data is being taken in paper form"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers and insiders often exfiltrate to personal instances of the same services the company uses. Tenant restrictions and instance-aware controls with DLP address this. Blocking everything breaks business."},
{"id":"o49","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing continuity for the organization's security tools themselves. The identity provider is cloud-hosted. Which scenario MOST needs to be planned for?","o":["An IdP outage or compromise locking admins out of security tools","The SIEM running out of disk space during a period of unusually high log volume, causing gaps in collection","A senior security analyst being on vacation during a major incident, slowing down the investigation","A vendor releasing a new major version of a security product that changes its management interface"],"a":[0],"e":"If all tools use SSO through the IdP, an IdP outage or compromise can lock responders out exactly when they're needed. Break-glass access and offline procedures are essential."},
{"id":"o50","d":6,"q":"An organization is defining its approach to asset management for security operations. Why is an accurate asset inventory critical?","o":["You can't monitor, patch, or protect assets you don't know about","It is only needed for financial depreciation schedules and for tracking the book value of hardware assets","It is only needed to make sure the organization has the right number of software licenses for each product","It is a compliance checkbox for auditors with no real operational value for the security operations team"],"a":[0],"e":"Asset inventory is foundational (CIS Controls 1 and 2). Unknown assets are unmonitored, unpatched, and often the entry point in breaches."},
{"id":"o51","d":6,"q":"A security operations team needs to contain a compromised laptop that is connected to a home network. Which capability is MOST effective?","o":["EDR network containment that isolates the endpoint wherever it is","Disable the user's VPN account so that the laptop can no longer reach any corporate resources from home","Call the user and ask them to unplug the laptop from the network and power it off immediately","Block the laptop's current public IP address at the corporate firewall and the cloud proxy"],"a":[0],"e":"EDR isolation works wherever the device is and keeps a channel for investigation. Disabling VPN doesn't stop internet access, relying on the user is slow and unreliable, and home IPs change."},
{"id":"o52","d":6,"q":"A company is establishing its crisis management structure. Which statement BEST describes the relationship between the incident response team and the crisis management team?","o":["IR handles technical response; crisis management handles strategy, communications, and business impact","They are the same team under different names, and the incident commander leads both technical and business decisions","The crisis team handles technical containment and eradication, while the IR team handles media inquiries","The incident response team reports directly to the media and regulators during a major incident"],"a":[0],"e":"Major incidents need both technical and business leadership. Clear interfaces between IR and crisis management prevent confusion over who decides what, especially external communications."},
{"id":"o53","d":6,"q":"An architect is evaluating the use of extended detection and response (XDR). What is the PRIMARY benefit over separate point tools?","o":["Correlated detection and response across endpoint, identity, email, network, and cloud","It eliminates the need to collect or retain any logs, because detections happen inside the XDR platform","It replaces all preventive controls such as firewalls, email filtering, and endpoint hardening","It works without any data sources, because detections are based on the vendor's global threat intelligence"],"a":[0],"e":"XDR integrates telemetry across domains to detect multi-stage attacks and coordinate response. It doesn't replace logging, prevention, or data sources."},
{"id":"o54","d":6,"q":"A company plans to purchase cyber insurance. The insurer requires a specific incident response retainer firm. How should the architect incorporate this into the IR plan?","o":["Build the firm into the IR plan with pre-agreed engagement steps, and confirm insurer notification rules","Ignore the insurer's requirement and rely only on the internal incident response team for all incidents","Notify the insurer only after the incident has been fully resolved and all costs are known","Wait to engage the retainer firm until a lawsuit has been filed against the company"],"a":[0],"e":"Insurance terms often require prompt notification and use of panel vendors. Pre-integration avoids delays and coverage disputes. Late notification can void coverage."},
{"id":"o55","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing monitoring for privileged account activity. Which control provides the BEST combination of prevention and detection?","o":["PAM with session recording, high-risk command alerts, and correlation with change tickets","Logging of privileged logins only, with the logs reviewed by the security team at the end of each month","A monthly review of the list of privileged accounts by each system owner, with sign-off recorded","Shared privileged accounts, so that there are fewer accounts that need to be monitored and reviewed"],"a":[0],"e":"Session controls, command alerting, and change correlation give both oversight and detection. Login logging lacks detail, monthly reviews are slow, and shared accounts remove accountability."},
{"id":"o56","d":6,"q":"An organization is designing a vulnerability disclosure response for its own products. A researcher reports a vulnerability and plans to publish in 90 days. What is the BEST practice?","o":["Acknowledge promptly, agree a fix timeline, keep them updated, and coordinate the fix and advisory","Ignore the researcher until the 90 days have passed, then respond to any public disclosure as it happens","Threaten legal action against the researcher to prevent publication of the vulnerability details","Fix the issue silently in the next release without acknowledging the researcher or issuing an advisory"],"a":[0],"e":"Coordinated vulnerability disclosure builds trust and protects users. Ignoring or threatening researchers often leads to uncoordinated disclosure and reputational harm."},
{"id":"o57","d":6,"q":"A company's SOC uses a SIEM with detection rules written years ago. The environment has since moved largely to SaaS and cloud. What is the MOST likely problem?","o":["Coverage gaps: old rules target on-premises sources while cloud and SaaS sources go uncovered","The SIEM will become faster, because fewer of the old rules will match any incoming events","The existing rules will automatically adapt to the new cloud and SaaS log sources over time","There is no problem, because well-written detection rules apply equally to every environment"],"a":[0],"e":"Detection must evolve with the environment. Old rules may not see cloud activity. Regular detection engineering reviews keep coverage aligned with the current architecture and threats."},
{"id":"o58","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing the recovery of Active Directory after a complete compromise. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["A documented forest recovery from clean offline backups","Restore the latest domain controller backup and reconnect it to the network immediately","Reset only user passwords and continue normal operations","Rename all of the domain admin accounts so the attacker can't find them"],"a":[0],"e":"AD compromise needs careful forest recovery: clean backups from before the compromise, an isolated restore, and comprehensive credential resets to remove persistence. Partial measures leave the attacker in control."},
{"id":"o59","d":6,"q":"An organization wants to measure the maturity of its security operations. Which framework is MOST appropriate?","o":["SOC-CMM","OWASP Top 10, applied to the SOC's own web-based tools and portals","Common Criteria, applied to each tool the SOC uses for monitoring","PCI DSS, applied to the SOC's logging and monitoring requirements"],"a":[0],"e":"SOC-CMM assesses security operations across business, people, process, technology, and services domains. OWASP, Common Criteria, and PCI serve other purposes."},
{"id":"o60","d":6,"q":"A company's DR plan relies on a single cloud region for both production and backups. What is the MOST significant risk?","o":["One regional outage or account compromise could hit production and backups together","Users will experience higher network latency when accessing applications in that region","Storage costs will be lower than if backups were kept in a second region","Failover will perform better because backups are close to production"],"a":[0],"e":"Shared failure domains (region or account) mean one event can take out production and backups together. Cross-region and cross-account backup storage, with immutability, reduces this risk."},
{"id":"o61","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing the process for handling security alerts from cloud workload protection tools. Development teams own the cloud accounts. Which operating model is MOST effective?","o":["Route alerts to owning teams with SLAs, while the SOC watches high-severity items and escalates misses","Have the central SOC fix every cloud misconfiguration directly in every account","Send all alerts to a shared email inbox for whoever has time","Ignore cloud alerts, since developers own the accounts and are responsible for them"],"a":[0],"e":"A federated model with ownership, SLAs, and central oversight scales across many teams. Central teams can't fix everything, shared inboxes get ignored, and ignoring alerts leaves risk unmanaged."},
{"id":"o62","d":6,"q":"During an incident, analysts discover that the attacker used a zero-day vulnerability in a VPN appliance. What should the response architecture support?","o":["Rapid compensating controls, hunting, and coordination","Wait for the vendor to release a patch before taking any action","Disclose the vulnerability publicly on social media at once","Replace the entire network infrastructure with another vendor's products"],"a":[0],"e":"Zero-days need immediate mitigation without a patch, plus hunting for compromise and coordinated sharing (such as through an ISAC). Waiting leaves exposure, and premature public disclosure can arm other attackers."},
{"id":"o63","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing logging for a high-volume web application. Which log events are MOST important for security monitoring?","o":["Authentication, authorization and validation failures, privilege changes, and sensitive data access","Every page view, including requests for static images, stylesheets, and scripts, with full headers","CPU and memory statistics from every web server, sampled once per second","Debug traces for every function call, including parameters and return values"],"a":[0],"e":"Security-relevant events show attack attempts and misuse. Logging every static request or debug trace produces volume without value and may leak sensitive data."},
{"id":"o64","d":6,"q":"An organization wants to share threat intelligence with industry peers. Which framework helps ensure shared information is handled appropriately?","o":["Traffic Light Protocol markings, with STIX/TAXII","CVSS scoring of every indicator before it is shared, so recipients know how severe each one is","The OWASP Top 10 categories, used to tag each shared indicator","ISO 9001 quality management procedures for handling shared documents"],"a":[0],"e":"TLP (CLEAR, GREEN, AMBER, AMBER+STRICT, RED) tells recipients how widely they may share information. STIX and TAXII standardize the format and transport of threat intelligence."},
{"id":"o65","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing an approach to handle end-of-life systems that can no longer receive security patches but must remain in operation. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Isolate, restrict, monitor, accept formally, replace","Keep them on the general network without any changes until they fail","Disconnect them from every network, making them unusable for the business","Install the latest antivirus and consider the risk resolved"],"a":[0],"e":"Unpatchable systems need layered compensating controls and a documented risk decision with a replacement plan. Leaving them exposed invites compromise, and antivirus alone doesn't fix unpatched vulnerabilities."},
{"id":"o66","d":6,"q":"A global logistics company has three regional SOCs that each use different SIEMs, playbooks, and severity definitions. During a recent multi-region incident, each SOC rated the same activity differently and no one coordinated the response for 18 hours. What is the BEST architectural improvement?","o":["A common taxonomy, severity model, and global escalation with an incident commander, feeding shared case management","Replace all three SIEMs with one product immediately and close the regional SOCs to centralize all staff in one location","Let each regional SOC keep operating independently, since local teams understand their own regions best","Assign every incident globally to whichever regional SOC currently has the most analysts on shift"],"a":[0],"e":"The failure was in coordination and shared definitions, not tooling. A common taxonomy, severity model, and command structure fixes cross-region response, and shared case management gives one view. Rip-and-replace is costly and slow, and independent SOCs repeat the failure."},
{"id":"o67","d":6,"q":"An organization detects that an attacker has exfiltrated customer personal data. Which activity should the incident response team ensure happens EARLY, in parallel with technical containment?","o":["Engage legal and privacy teams to assess notification duties and deadlines","Wait until the investigation is fully complete before involving the legal team, so that they receive accurate facts","Publicly announce the breach before scoping it, to demonstrate transparency to customers and regulators","Delete logs showing the full extent of the breach to limit the organization's legal exposure"],"a":[0],"e":"Notification clocks (such as 72 hours under GDPR) may start when the organization becomes aware. Early legal involvement ensures timely, accurate notifications. Premature announcements may be wrong, and destroying evidence is unlawful."},
{"id":"o68","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing the use of canary files to detect ransomware. How do canary files help?","o":["Decoy files whose modification triggers immediate alerts and containment","They encrypt real files before ransomware can reach them, so the attacker can't encrypt them again","They automatically back up every file to a protected location as soon as it is modified","They slow down the CPU when encryption is detected, so ransomware can't finish its work"],"a":[0],"e":"Canary files act as tripwires: legitimate users have no reason to touch them, so changes signal ransomware or unauthorized access early, enabling fast containment."},
{"id":"o69","d":6,"q":"A company's DR plan was written five years ago and hasn't been updated, although the company has since moved half its systems to the cloud. What is the MOST significant risk?","o":["It no longer reflects current dependencies and procedures, so it may fail","It will have grown too long for staff to read during an actual disaster declaration","It will use outdated templates and formatting that no longer match corporate document standards","There is no risk, because disaster recovery plans don't need updating once they are approved"],"a":[0],"e":"DR plans must be maintained as the environment changes, with updates triggered by significant changes and regular testing. Out-of-date plans give false confidence."},
{"id":"o70","d":6,"q":"A security operations architect is selecting a log format standard to simplify parsing across diverse sources. Which approach is MOST useful?","o":["Normalize to a common schema such as OCSF or ECS at ingestion, keeping raw data","Store each source's logs in whatever native format it produces, with no normalization at any stage","Convert all logs to PDF documents so that they are easier for auditors and analysts to read","Store only summary counts for each source and discard the raw events after one day"],"a":[0],"e":"Normalization enables consistent detection rules and correlation across sources, and keeping raw data preserves forensic fidelity. Unnormalized data complicates correlation, and summaries lose detail."},
{"id":"o71","d":6,"q":"An organization experiences a DDoS attack while also seeing unusual login activity on its admin portal. What should the incident response team consider?","o":["The DDoS may be a diversion, so investigate the logins in parallel","Ignore the login activity for now, because the DDoS attack is the more urgent issue to deal with","Shut down every system until the DDoS has ended, so that attackers can't reach anything","Assume the unusual login activity is just a side effect of the DDoS traffic hitting the portal"],"a":[0],"e":"Attackers sometimes use DDoS as a smokescreen. Responders should investigate concurrent suspicious activity rather than fixating on the obvious attack."},
{"id":"o72","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing secure log access for the SOC. Logs contain personal data. Which control is MOST appropriate?","o":["Least-privilege, masked, audited log access","Give every employee read access to the logs for transparency","Unrestricted analyst access with no auditing","Encrypt the logs with a key that nobody holds"],"a":[0],"e":"Logs are sensitive data themselves. Least privilege, masking, and auditing of searches protect privacy while allowing investigations. Encrypting with an inaccessible key makes the logs useless."},
{"id":"o73","d":6,"q":"A company needs to recover from a major outage. The DR plan lists 40 applications, all labeled 'critical.' Recovery staff don't know where to start. What is the root problem?","o":["The BIA didn't tier applications by impact, priority, and dependencies","There are too few recovery staff to restore 40 applications at the same time during an outage","The applications are too old to be restored quickly with modern recovery tools and procedures","The DR site is too far away from the primary site for staff to reach quickly during an outage"],"a":[0],"e":"If everything is critical, nothing is prioritized. A proper BIA tiers applications by impact and maps dependencies so recovery follows a defined order."},
{"id":"o74","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing detection for lateral movement in a Windows environment. Which data source is MOST valuable?","o":["Remote logon and process-creation events with command lines, plus EDR telemetry on remote execution","Printer logs from every networked printer, collected centrally by the print management server","DHCP lease logs only, showing which IP address was assigned to each device at each time","Web proxy logs only, showing which internal hosts accessed which external websites"],"a":[0],"e":"Lateral movement shows up as remote logons, remote service creation, and remote execution tools (PsExec, WMI, WinRM). Authentication and process events with EDR telemetry capture these."},
{"id":"o75","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing a process to verify that security patches have been successfully applied across all servers. Which approach is MOST reliable?","o":["Independent verification by authenticated scans or configuration reports against the inventory","Trust the patch deployment tool's success report, since it confirms each installation directly with the endpoint agent","Ask each system administrator to confirm by email that their servers have been patched","Assume patches were applied successfully if the deployment tool reported no errors"],"a":[0],"e":"Deployment tools can report success when patches didn't actually take effect (pending reboot, failed install). Independent verification against the full inventory catches gaps."},
{"id":"o76","d":6,"q":"An organization relies on a single managed security service provider for all monitoring. The MSSP suffers a breach. What should the organization's architecture have included?","o":["Limited, monitored MSSP access, breach notification terms, fast revocation, and an exit plan","Unlimited administrative access for the MSSP across every system, so that it can respond to incidents without delay","No monitoring of the MSSP's own activity, since the MSSP is the organization's monitor","A single shared account for all MSSP analysts, so that access is easy to manage"],"a":[0],"e":"Security providers are high-value targets with privileged access. Their access must be least privilege, monitored, revocable, and contractually governed. Unlimited or unmonitored access magnifies the impact of a provider breach."},
{"id":"o77","d":6,"q":"A company wants to ensure that its incident response plan covers incidents in SaaS applications it does not control. What should the plan include?","o":["Provider contacts and escalation, knowledge of available logs and actions, and pre-configured log export","Nothing, because SaaS providers handle every security incident that affects their platform and their customers","A plan to physically visit the SaaS provider's data center during an incident","A requirement that every SaaS provider follow the company's own incident response plan"],"a":[0],"e":"In SaaS, the customer still owns identities, configuration, and data use. Plans must know what logs exist, how to export them, and how to engage the provider."},
{"id":"o78","d":6,"q":"An architect is designing monitoring to detect cryptojacking (unauthorized cryptocurrency mining) in cloud environments. Which signal is MOST indicative?","o":["Unexpected compute creation or sustained high CPU/GPU use, with connections to mining pools","A sudden decrease in object storage usage across the organization's cloud accounts","An increase in successful logins by known administrators during normal business hours","An increase in visitors to the company's public website from new countries"],"a":[0],"e":"Cryptojacking consumes compute and connects to mining pools. Cloud billing anomalies, resource creation in unusual regions, and network indicators are key signals."},
{"id":"o79","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing security operations for an organization that must comply with a 'right to audit' clause from major customers. Which capability BEST supports this?","o":["Well-organized evidence of control operation, provided securely without exposing other customers","Unrestricted customer access to production systems, so that auditors can verify any control directly","A policy of refusing all audit requests to protect the confidentiality of other customers","Providing audit evidence verbally in meetings, so that nothing sensitive is written down"],"a":[0],"e":"Prepared, scoped evidence supports audits efficiently while protecting other customers. Unrestricted access creates risk, refusal breaches contracts, and verbal evidence isn't evidence."},
{"id":"o80","d":6,"q":"A company's security team wants to know whether attackers are already present in the network, despite no alerts. Which activity is MOST appropriate?","o":["A compromise assessment (hunting plus forensic sweeps)","A compliance audit against ISO 27001, covering every Annex A control in scope","An authenticated vulnerability scan of all internal and external assets","A review of the organization's security policies and standards"],"a":[0],"e":"Compromise assessments specifically look for evidence of existing intrusions. Compliance audits, scans, and policy reviews assess controls and weaknesses, not presence of attackers."},
{"id":"o81","d":6,"q":"An organization is designing the communication plan for a data breach. Which principle is MOST important?","o":["Accurate, timely, consistent, approved, and coordinated with legal obligations","Communicate as little as possible to avoid liability, even when the law requires notification","Let each department communicate independently with its own customers and partners","Speculate publicly about who the attacker might be, to show the company is on top of it"],"a":[0],"e":"Coordinated, accurate communication protects stakeholders and reputation, and meets legal requirements. Withholding required notices, inconsistent messages, and speculation cause harm."},
{"id":"o82","d":6,"q":"A security architect is evaluating the resilience of the SOC's own infrastructure. Which threat is MOST important to consider?","o":["Attackers targeting security tools and the SOC itself to blind defenders","Hardware failure of an analyst's monitor during a busy shift in the SOC","The temperature in the SOC rising above the comfortable range for analysts","SOC staff using personal phones on the SOC floor during their shifts"],"a":[0],"e":"Sophisticated attackers try to blind defenders. Protecting SOC infrastructure with strong access control, integrity monitoring, and alerting on logging gaps is critical."},
{"id":"o83","d":6,"q":"An organization is subject to a regulation that requires reporting significant incidents within 4 hours of classification. Which design element BEST supports compliance?","o":["Predefined criteria and decision trees, templates, pre-assigned approvers, and clock tracking","Ask the CEO to write each regulatory report from scratch once an incident is classified","Wait for the full root cause analysis before classifying the incident at all","Classify every incident as minor to avoid triggering the reporting requirement"],"a":[0],"e":"Tight reporting windows need preparation: clear criteria, templates, approvers, and tracking. Writing from scratch or waiting for root cause is too slow, and misclassifying incidents is non-compliant."},
{"id":"o84","d":6,"q":"A company is designing its approach to managing security exceptions for vulnerability remediation SLAs. Which approach is MOST appropriate?","o":["Owned, justified, time-limited exceptions","Exceptions granted automatically whenever a team requests one","No exceptions permitted under any circumstances","Informal tracking of exceptions in each team's chat channel"],"a":[0],"e":"Structured exceptions keep risk visible and temporary. Automatic grants and informal tracking hide risk, and prohibiting exceptions leads teams to ignore SLAs."},
{"id":"o85","d":6,"q":"An organization's SOC detects malware on a laptop belonging to the CFO. The CFO is traveling and demands that the laptop not be isolated before a critical board presentation. What should the incident handler do?","o":["Escalate to the right authority with alternatives","Follow the CFO's instruction without escalating, since executives can accept risk","Isolate the laptop immediately without telling the CFO","Ignore the alert until the CFO returns"],"a":[0],"e":"Executives aren't exempt from risk processes, but business impact is real. Escalating to the decision authority with alternatives balances both. Unilateral action either way, or ignoring the alert, is poor practice."},
{"id":"o86","d":6,"q":"A company uses a cloud provider's native backup service with backups stored in the same account as production. Which improvement BEST protects against account compromise?","o":["An isolated, immutable backup account","Increase the backup frequency so that more recovery points are available","Encrypt backups with the production account's key","Tag backups 'do not delete'"],"a":[0],"e":"Account isolation ensures a compromised production account can't delete backups. Immutability adds protection. More frequent backups and production keys don't address account-level compromise, and tags aren't access controls."},
{"id":"o87","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing a metrics dashboard for executives about security operations. Which set of metrics is MOST appropriate?","o":["Outcome and risk metrics tied to business impact","Firewall packets dropped and antivirus signature counts","Raw SIEM events per second","Every open ticket with full technical detail"],"a":[0],"e":"Executives need outcome and risk-oriented metrics linked to business impact. Packet counts, EPS, and ticket lists are operational detail."},
{"id":"o88","d":6,"q":"An organization discovers that its incident response playbooks assume the SOC has access to corporate laptops through the VPN, but most employees now work remotely without VPN. What should be updated?","o":["Use cloud-managed EDR, MDM, and identity controls that work on any network","Require all employees to return to the office so that the existing playbooks work again as written","Remove the playbooks entirely, since they are outdated and no longer reflect how people work","Ask employees to connect to the VPN when an incident occurs so the SOC can reach their laptops"],"a":[0],"e":"Response capabilities must match the working model. Cloud-managed tools allow investigation and containment anywhere. Relying on VPN connection during an incident is unreliable."},
{"id":"o89","d":6,"q":"A security architect is designing a process to ensure lessons learned from incidents result in actual improvements. Which approach is MOST effective?","o":["Track action items with owners and due dates, and verify implementation","Write a detailed report after each incident and store it on the shared drive for the team to read","Discuss the lessons in a meeting with everyone involved, without writing anything down","Rely on the memory of the staff who handled the incident to apply the lessons next time"],"a":[0],"e":"Lessons only become improvements if tracked to completion and verified. Reports without follow-up and undocumented discussions rarely lead to change."},
{"id":"o90","d":6,"q":"An organization is designing security monitoring for its use of generative AI services. Which risk is MOST important to monitor?","o":["Sensitive data sent to unapproved AI services, and anomalous activity by approved AI agents","The amount of electricity consumed by AI services used by employees across the organization","The number of employees who say in surveys that they find AI tools useful in their daily work","Changes to the color theme and branding of AI chat interfaces used within the company"],"a":[0],"e":"Key AI-related security risks include data leakage to unsanctioned services and misuse of AI agents with access to internal data and tools. Monitoring through CASB, DLP, and agent activity logs addresses these."}
);
